{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For standard form: move the decimal point until A is between 1 and 10",
   "Count places moved — this gives the power n (right = positive, left = negative)",
   "To multiply in standard form: multiply A values, add powers, adjust if needed",
   "To divide: divide A values, subtract powers, adjust if needed"
  ],
  "title": "How to Use Powers, Roots and Standard Form",
  "content": "<p><strong>Powers (indices)</strong> are a shorthand for repeated multiplication: \\(a^n\\) means \\(a\\) multiplied by itself \\(n\\) times. Key rules: \\(a^0 = 1\\), \\(a^1 = a\\).</p><p><strong>Square roots</strong> (\\(\\sqrt{}\\)) and <strong>cube roots</strong> (\\(\\sqrt[3]{}\\)) are the inverse of squaring and cubing. You should know the squares 1² to 15² and the cubes 1³ to 5³.</p><p><strong>Standard form</strong> writes numbers as \\(A \\times 10^n\\) where \\(1 \\le A < 10\\) and \\(n\\) is an integer. Large numbers have positive \\(n\\); small numbers (between 0 and 1) have negative \\(n\\).</p><p><strong>Calculating in standard form:</strong> Multiply/divide the \\(A\\) values and add/subtract the powers. Adjust \\(A\\) if it falls outside \\([1, 10)\\).</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Write 0.00045 in standard form</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Move decimal right until we get \\(4.5\\) (a value between 1 and 10)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> We moved 4 places right, so \\(n = -4\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(4.5 \\times 10^{-4}\\)</p>"
 },
